    "q-18494": {
      "dom_id": "step-q18494",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Which of the following could be the graph of a function $f(x)$ that satisfies the conditions below?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(-1)={f}^{″}(-1)=0$\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,\\infty )$\n\n- if $x\\in (-\\infty ,-1)$ then ${f}^{″}(x)<0$\n\n- if $x\\in (-1,\\infty )$ then ${f}^{″}(x)>0$",
      "worked_solution": "We summarize all the given information in the following table:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| ${f}^{″}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗\\,\\,⌢$ | $\\text{not extr.}$ | $↗\\,\\,⌣$ |\n\nNow, let's sketch our graph: \n\n- We draw a point on the vertical line $x=-1$, where the tangent to the graph is horizontal.\n\n- The graph must go down to the left of $x=-1$ and go up to the right of that point.\n\n- Finally, the graph must be concave down on $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and concave up on $(-1,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, a possible graph of $f$ could be as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14193742/assets/c7e780bcf84d38b5604e64a7285b9bd591c0520049c0fe288c0a9735df5d5454.png)",
