    "q-51129": {
      "dom_id": "step-q51129",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Which of the following could be the graph of a function $f(x)$ that satisfies the conditions below?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)>0$, for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n\n- $f(2)=2.$",
      "worked_solution": "We summarize all the given information in the following table: \n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ |  | $2$ |  |\n\nThis gives us the following information about the graph of $f:$\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $2$ | $↗$ |\n\nSo, we can conclude that\n\n- the graph of $y=f(x)$ must pass through the point $(2,2),$ and\n\n- $f(x)$ is increasing on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each graph:\n\n|  | I | II | III | IV | V |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Passes through $(2,2)$ | $✓$ | $✓$ | $✓$ | $✓$ | $✓$ |\n| $↗$ on $(-\\infty ,\\infty )$ | $\\times $ | $✓$ | $\\times $ | $\\times $ | $\\times $ |\n\nTherefore, a possible graph of $f$ is as follows:  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14193742/assets/5d725bd68f0278e429a0aa2ab2d4a78942b75d6e5095c2d01158ae7dbd3cca9a.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
