    "q-117020": {
      "dom_id": "step-q117020",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Which of the following could be the graph of a function $f(x)$ that satisfies the conditions below?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(2)=0$\n\n- if $x\\in (-\\infty ,2)$ then ${f}^{′}(x)<0$\n\n- if $x\\in (2,\\infty )$ then ${f}^{″}(x)<0$",
      "worked_solution": "We summarize all the given information in the following table:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ |  |\n| ${f}^{″}$ |  |  | $-$ |\n| $f$ |  |  |  |\n\nThis gives us the following information about the graph of $f:$\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ |  |\n| ${f}^{″}$ |  |  | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | Zero slope | $⌢$ |\n\nSo, we can conclude that\n\n- the graph of $y=f(x)$ has a zero slope when $x=2,$\n\n- $f(x)$ is decreasing on $(-\\infty ,2),$ and\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each graph:\n\n|  | I | II | III | IV | V |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Zero slope when $x=2$ | $\\times $ | $\\times $ | $✓$ | $✓$ | $✓$ |\n| $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $✓$ | $✓$ | $✓$ | $\\times $ | $✓$ |\n| $⌢$ on $(2,\\infty )$ | $✓$ | $\\times $ | $✓$ | $✓$ | $\\times $ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, a possible graph of $f$ is as follows: \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14193742/assets/24cd872d8ab5d9d3e911afad64de02e541e2d877dae1e9f6925a5afa22e1f0fd.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
