    "q-117073": {
      "dom_id": "step-q117073",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Which of the following could be the graph of a continuous function $f(x)$ that satisfies the conditions below?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)$ and ${f}^{″}(x)$ are undefined at $x=1$\n\n- if $x\\in (-\\infty ,1)$ then ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ and if $x\\in (1,\\infty )$ then ${f}^{′}(x)<0$\n\n- ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )$",
      "worked_solution": "We summarize all the given information in the following table:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $-$ |\n| ${f}^{″}$ | $+$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗\\,\\,⌣$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘\\,\\,⌣$ |\n\nNow, let's sketch our graph: \n\n- We draw a point on the vertical line $x=1$, where the derivative is undefined.\n\n- Since $x=1$ is a local maximum, the graph must go down to the left and to the right of that point.\n\n- The graph must be concave up on $(-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$\n\n- Finally, we are told that the function is continuous.\n\nTherefore, a possible graph of $f$ could be as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14193742/assets/8c4aa28d2fe102609adcc6c9dbc9f7bd859b6feed443acc78694091fbec5241f.png)",
